[2026-04-03 02:27:10] Investigated the remaining SYSCTL SystemClock orchestration gap after closing SYSCTL_InterruptSource.c.
- Scope: TM4C129E_DriverLib/xDriver_MCU/SYSCTL/Driver/xSource/SYSCTL_SystemClock.c, with emphasis on SYSCTL__enSetSystemClock().
- Existing host coverage already in place:
  - null-pointer rejection for VCO and PLL frequency getters
  - PIOSC PLL reference path independent of MOSC state
  - MOSC-available and MOSC-unavailable runtime frequency helpers
  - PLL output divisor application in SYSCTL__enGetPLLClockFrequency()
- New adjacent production finding:
  - In the PLL configuration branch, SYSCTL__enSetSystemClock() currently calls SYSCTL__enGetPLLClockFrequency() before programming the new PLL divisor.
  - SYSCTL__enGetPLLClockFrequency() divides the VCO by the current `RSCLKCFG.PLLSYSDIV + 1`, so the later divisor calculation depends on stale hardware state if the incoming PLL divisor is nonzero.
  - This can cause SYSCTL__enSetPLLClockDivisor() to program the wrong final divisor even when the requested clock and VCO settings are otherwise valid.
- Decision:
  - fix the PLL branch to derive the requested divisor from the raw VCO frequency instead of the current PLL output frequency
  - extend SYSCTL_SystemClock_Test.cpp with orchestration-focused regression coverage, including a stale-divisor PLL case and a MOSC power-up timeout path.